@page "/docs/accessibility/color-and-motion"

@* Contrast (Color.cs, GrayPalette, BrandPalette incl. the light-mode focus fallback), charts' palette,
   forced colors (the sweep of Nov 10, 2026), reduced motion (inventory section 1.6). *@

<ArticlePage Section="accessibility" Slug="color-and-motion">
    <Lead>
        Text meets WCAG's 4.5:1 contrast in light and dark, and so does a brand color you set. In Windows high-contrast mode,
        components switch to the system's colors and keep their focus rings and indicators. When the user asks for reduced
        motion, components stop sliding and growing.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Contrast</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>Text has at least 4.5:1 contrast against its background in both color schemes; body text has 7:1. Disabled text is lighter, as WCAG allows.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>A field's border and a switch's track are about 2:1 against the page, and every field has a visible label above it. For strict 3:1, darken <Code>--ib-color-outline</Code>; see Known gaps in the <Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/overview">overview</Anchor>.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>A status, such as success or danger, is never color alone: it comes with an icon and a word.</ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Your own brand and gray</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>IzzyTheme</Code>'s <Code>Brand</Code> is checked when the page renders. Text on a brand-colored button keeps 4.5:1,
            by moving the fill's lightness when neither white nor dark text would. The brand as text, such as a link, keeps 4.5:1 on
            the page and on the gray fills. In dark mode a dark brand is lifted until it shows against the background.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            A light brand, such as a yellow, keeps its color on buttons and checked boxes, where dark text or a check mark carries
            the contrast. The focus ring switches to the darker brand text color, so it still has 3:1 against the page.
            <Code>Gray</Code> is checked the same way: muted text keeps 4.5:1. See <Anchor Href="docs/theming/themes">Themes and presets</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            A color you set directly, on a component's <Code>Color</Code> or in CSS, isn't checked. Keep the pairs in
            <Anchor Href="docs/theming/tokens">Tokens and customizing</Anchor> together and check them yourself.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Charts</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Lines don't rely on color alone: they have dash patterns and markers of different shapes. Bars and pie slices differ
            by color, and the legend, tooltips and Show data table name every series and slice. A few colors of the default palette (pink, yellow, aqua and orange) are
            under 3:1 against a white page, so a chart that needs them should keep its legend or labels.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Windows high-contrast mode</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            In high-contrast mode (forced colors) the browser replaces the page's colors with the user's system colors and removes
            backgrounds and shadows. IzzyBlazor handles it:
        </TextBlock>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>Every focusable control shows its focus with an outline, which the system colors keep.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Checkboxes, radios and switches fall back to the browser's own drawing.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Indicators drawn as fills, such as a progress bar, a slider's track, a carousel's dots, a selected tab or a chosen option, switch to the system's highlight color.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Charts keep their own colors, so the series can still be told apart.</ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Reduced motion</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            When the user's system asks for reduced motion, components stop sliding, growing and turning:
        </TextBlock>
        <List Gap="1">
            <ListItem>Dialogs and drawers open and close without sliding, and so do popovers, menus and tooltips.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Accordions, collapsibles, tabs and the sidebar change at once.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Toasts appear without sliding in, and a carousel changes slides at once and doesn't start autoplay.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Rows, list items and DataGrid columns that make way for a drag jump to their places.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Chevrons don't turn, and the chat's typing indicator stops.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Spinners and progress bars slow down rather than stop, since their motion means "still working".</ListItem>
        </List>
        <TextBlock>
            IzzyBlazor has no page-wide reset, so your own animations are yours to handle with
            <Code>@@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>
